s participate in 13 static triangles in [43373, 55373] (step_03) \u2014 so an \"ignore time\" view would suggest closure. But at \u03b4=800, **0 of 13** triangles closed promptly (step_04), with a median closing lag of **3010** time units \u2014 nearly 4\u00d7 the required threshold.\n\n**Threshold sweep shows the lags are structurally long, not marginally late:**\n- \u03b4=800 \u2192 0/13 closed (step_04)\n- \u03b4=1500 \u2192 0/13 closed (step_09)\n- \u03b4=2000 \u2192 1/13 (7.7%) (step_06)\n- \u03b4=4000 \u2192 9/13 (69%) (step_07)\n\nThe closure lags cluster in the ~1600\u20133600 range (step_07 examples: 3010, 2582, 2831, 2801, 2649, 3601, 3087, 2633). Closures do eventually happen, but they lag their supporting wedges by roughly 3\u20134\u00d7 the wave criterion. This is the signature of *background* triangle formation, not a prompt wave.\n\n**Confounder controls:**\n- *Hub artifact:* The single sub-2000 closure (80\u2013332\u20131583, lag 1645) runs through node 1583, which has **117 distinct partners** in the window (step_12). A hub of that degree manufactures wedges and incidental closures by volume; this is not evidence of a coordinated wave among the target nodes.\n- *Volume/popularity:* The target nodes are sparse and non-repeating \u2014 2\u20135 events each, essentially all distinct partners (step_05), against a window-wide 2853 events and repeat ratio 0.0137 (step_01). None appear among the top-15 active nodes (step_02).\n- *Hidden sub-burst:* Activity is flat across 12 bins (2\u20136 events per bin, step_08), and burst detection at z=2 finds **no bursts** (step_13). Restricting to the densest sub-window [46000, 53000] yields zero qualifying triangles at \u03b4=800 (step_10).\n\n**Conclusion: absent.** Connectivity is real but temporally diffuse; no prompt closure wave.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_1852ce27\",\n  \"decision\": \"absent\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"closure_wave\",\n  \"entities\": [6, 10, 17, 37, 57, 72, 80, 87, 106, 111, 114, 116],\n  